#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078245 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "63a9a3bd-b7ce-5c1d-a138-0bc8f8f87ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-536" :question/problem "For the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2,\n$$\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2.$ We also know that $y'(x) = 2xy,$ so\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(4)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$ using $L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "45166435-4bf0-5356-baa3-436258817620" :question/math-academy-id "e-7700" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "53e498a9-cb99-57bf-9b24-3cf67dc9a021" :question/math-academy-id "e-9340" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c20005d0-3ecf-5645-9bb4-9c889a7f6dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296575} {:answer/id #uuid "417f3f92-6846-5a06-a515-266de84ad570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296576} {:answer/id #uuid "7b750dca-e8c4-5067-99fb-2ecc269caa14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296577} {:answer/id #uuid "24dd0141-76a2-58ed-afde-9e05adc9878a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296578} {:answer/id #uuid "faeed072-7ad4-5468-94b5-190341d9e4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24dd0141-76a2-58ed-afde-9e05adc9878a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296578} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ef84c0-50c3-56a7-9f75-d2f7ff3269b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57291efe-f564-592a-a9de-0577f6c788bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-116777" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3xy,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3xy \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035ad812-d60e-5624-b8a1-15a9fd0b4e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296582} {:answer/id #uuid "08a9a459-4948-55cc-9622-8e3cefa7629b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296583} {:answer/id #uuid "a8441c90-ec4d-5b26-8b1c-add9da942246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296584} {:answer/id #uuid "c01d5035-a99b-5b33-9bd4-9161c48b289a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296585} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6e60dc-566f-53f8-a4de-0fdcb9d114f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08a9a459-4948-55cc-9622-8e3cefa7629b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296583} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2fe140c-06ed-5773-8e8d-ac5512927bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b364b0f5-ec74-5582-a4f8-a86d928ca115" :question/math-academy-id "q-116780" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-2y,\\,y(2)=4\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-2y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+(-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "436e0636-9ba2-5909-8330-892cd48aa266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296589} {:answer/id #uuid "6708155d-f693-5223-a966-cbf38b9909b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296590} {:answer/id #uuid "68940920-5c89-52f6-8004-fd5830534714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296591} {:answer/id #uuid "75e12958-2e30-5c25-a3bb-2050811bf8a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296592} {:answer/id #uuid "09289a8a-2c92-596f-9a7f-a5e105868c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6708155d-f693-5223-a966-cbf38b9909b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296590} :answer-field/id #uuid "d16974ed-8138-526a-b641-400751bbdfc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e19f5b5-50fe-5f57-9c1a-575a675ff16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-116798" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-5)+4,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-5)+4 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (0-5)+4 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{3})≈2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76ebdbe7-52c1-5a27-b417-37840052afeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296596} {:answer/id #uuid "3133eb17-1525-525b-be15-adbb824ecedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296597} {:answer/id #uuid "53a4c6e0-a295-5b82-8a77-be085378b7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296598} {:answer/id #uuid "e65bd501-86e9-5056-86bb-366562b865f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296599} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa24d78-cf23-51a6-b3c5-3092c519d035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e65bd501-86e9-5056-86bb-366562b865f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296599} :answer-field/id #uuid "847f19cc-7ca7-5e3e-b743-8af494687406" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e8ca51ff-ff06-57a5-82c5-1a97ff16ec68" :question/math-academy-id "q-116800" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(2)={e}^{2}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{2}-2=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | ${e}^{2}+2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{5}{2})≈{e}^{2}+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c30820-7d88-5ce9-a7ac-9c8dbad916e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296603} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f882f9-c34d-57a2-be35-1ef96a091dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296604} {:answer/id #uuid "8893b873-04ff-510d-b6e2-c42f8d16686a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296605} {:answer/id #uuid "b27158f4-96b6-58c0-b5ae-261bf13e35b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606} {:answer/id #uuid "d66b81c4-2154-591b-a600-36d63529dc70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b27158f4-96b6-58c0-b5ae-261bf13e35b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606} :answer-field/id #uuid "936ff7ba-a588-5bb1-aaf8-a13eab50f591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6824a700-87bf-5b53-ae25-dd822c1b3e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-116850" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-y,\\,y(2)=0.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=2$ to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(2)=0.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-y,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(2)={2}^{2}-0=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =0+4(x-2) \\\\  & =4(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(3)$ using $L(3).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(3)≈L(3)=4(3-2)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3703c75d-05bf-501e-932c-8878e6e511e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296610} {:answer/id #uuid "b1855851-8175-5203-8793-19c7bf5fdb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296611} {:answer/id #uuid "36b08d4c-c8f9-587a-8da6-5bd5d0cb9627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296612} {:answer/id #uuid "50f1a6f5-22ed-5642-a472-7c26554237f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296613} {:answer/id #uuid "3cee2472-a258-5030-804f-6fcd48e75c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50f1a6f5-22ed-5642-a472-7c26554237f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296613} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c859856-42f1-5308-9f5a-62046f0bf3c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0029ab2-56eb-5fc8-ad4a-cb97ed1d41e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116876" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy+1,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+1 \\\\  & =1(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2f9918d-fa1a-5c07-9371-485af0feb143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296617} {:answer/id #uuid "9075fece-7dbe-5985-b1ba-332ab1c9dc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9075fece-7dbe-5985-b1ba-332ab1c9dc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296618} :answer-field/id #uuid "20c9fdc7-199f-5aa1-9f1c-b95b30274e80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be07587-b2cb-55a6-8ee8-65d4e7dd1f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-347888" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-1),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2(0-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =2+(-2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfba522-30b6-558c-a3c7-9e31afe60347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621} {:answer/id #uuid "7190b1fe-1a01-575f-a664-655fb6e6081f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bfba522-30b6-558c-a3c7-9e31afe60347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f0a707-54eb-5c5f-8756-edbaca0ecbcd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bdbce26-43e4-57ca-abcb-2eb41a6f32af" :question/math-academy-id "q-347893" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(2)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(2)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4(2)-2(3) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9a069c9-05f4-5962-8e4f-990a822bdf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a069c9-05f4-5962-8e4f-990a822bdf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625} :answer-field/id #uuid "649fe6d2-57d0-521b-9de9-0eb0402e3951" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c92129c-0038-56dd-8216-6aeaf961bef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353287" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5455fc7f-2c66-5ef4-8ca0-cbe923316855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296628} {:answer/id #uuid "39f844c0-6a1c-52d3-97da-8aab223852cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5455fc7f-2c66-5ef4-8ca0-cbe923316855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296628} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5cff56-8af0-5362-8946-165cbaaa1c80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36019322-a793-597c-86ab-fb268e6382f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-353288" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{y},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{2}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d199cb-99ec-5a4a-82a2-bce972877741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296632} {:answer/id #uuid "e58a5217-8a6e-57a1-bd6b-8a6c2230977d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06d199cb-99ec-5a4a-82a2-bce972877741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296632} :answer-field/id #uuid "2027fe92-d80a-5a1b-9401-90779bb7edd3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99d8bba7-2e1f-51fa-ac81-818904c17603" :question/math-academy-id "q-353296" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+y \\\\  & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e4c48a5-ebc1-5bf3-8a84-aed377751f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e4c48a5-ebc1-5bf3-8a84-aed377751f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296636} :answer-field/id #uuid "16effb47-ed8a-597a-9dad-ba0bfd1cabb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aaa01801-5d77-55e8-8927-448f9b008b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353301" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\sqrt{x+{y}^{2}-3},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{0+{2}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1dc547d-3d83-5f77-8be5-d43ddebebce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1dc547d-3d83-5f77-8be5-d43ddebebce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296639} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a0ca86-a16d-5362-92e6-b106e12b3d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0af89621-bebd-5f7a-9c7e-95e56fb429cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-353306" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x+1},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Fill in the empty box in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{2y}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)}{0+1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{2})≈4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86bee354-eded-5d9a-b0a7-f32c77cd34b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076996} {:answer/id #uuid "c03b31fa-49e1-56d7-bbf5-fc10f5f27918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076997} {:answer/id #uuid "460765da-0359-51f6-a5b8-1fb2ddd098a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076998} {:answer/id #uuid "4dddb5e9-6375-5eae-966e-ad326375a496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076999} {:answer/id #uuid "60fd45dd-105d-52b6-89d8-b24be6f4cd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86bee354-eded-5d9a-b0a7-f32c77cd34b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076996} :answer-field/id #uuid "a86dbc34-dbf5-5b63-9e60-9d494ebe026b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f352ca6-2003-56c9-a6e6-ea5f1e44d59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-50119" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-4),\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=1.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-4),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=1(0-4)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =1+(-4)(x-0) \\\\  & =1-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(1)≈L(1)=1-4(1)=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f657aa48-4fe5-5263-8e6e-ca1309e637c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296642} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9bf9d1-ea8b-5edf-998e-d91db24a7b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296643} {:answer/id #uuid "d87fc9c8-28fb-578c-8bdb-3d6ab15475aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296644} {:answer/id #uuid "758885db-781e-5118-abdb-f99902324f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296645} {:answer/id #uuid "0761b8f6-ae5a-547e-b5c9-f5ebc8a97aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0761b8f6-ae5a-547e-b5c9-f5ebc8a97aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296646} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e56978-2f2a-5c18-8b95-6bc43e6934a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38ff9bfc-18eb-5b5e-9da9-d705a59355e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86973" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+5y,\\,y(-3)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=3?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(-3)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x+5y \\\\  & =-(-3)+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $24$ |"}]}
